     "result_text_tail": "d found no wrong values. I applied its fixes: five places where the wording overstated the evidence, and a waterfall chart that didn't quite add up.\n- **No \"?\" values remain.** All numbers rest on the briefing pack alone, so none could be confirmed by a second source.\n- **Hypothesis verdicts:** of the 11 hypotheses tested, 4 held, 6 held only in revised form and 1 was disproven (the onboarding plan halving SMB churn).\n\n**Choices I made for you, since you were unavailable (all easy to change):**\n- **Format:** the deep, 30\u201340 slide version, because this is a CEO decision; navy theme; written for the CEO and leadership team approving the 2027 plan.\n- **Out of scope:** a cheaper SMB plan against Inkwell, usage-based pricing, renegotiating enterprise caps and the new-customer sales plan.\n- **Planning values:**\n  - Mid-market: +10%, the level tested in 2024.\n  - Enterprise: +10% where uncapped, 7% where contracts cap rises.\n  - SMB test: +8% on a random half of non-promotion renewals, January to March.\n  - SMB sensitivity table: churn response from 0.5 to 3 times the 2023 level.\n\n**Two gaps the pack doesn't cover**\n- **New-customer ARR for 2026:** the deck shows the $0.6M needed rather than a forecast. Comparing it with actual 2026 bookings would confirm the 15% case.\n- **Mid-market churn by reason and deal size:** this would show whether Inkwell is behind the mid-market rise.\n\nI can switch the theme, change any of the planning values, or turn the deck into a shorter board version."
